    "answer": {
      "short": "Yes. $y'=kf'(x)$.",
      "worked": "With $u=k$ and $v=f(x)$, $u'=0$ and $v'=f'(x)$.\n$$\\begin{align*}y'&=u'v+uv'\\\\y'&=0\\cdot f(x)+kf'(x)\\\\y'&=kf'(x).\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
